Hi Armin,

Thanks for looking at this. The acpidump of the A715-79G (BIOS
1.07.01TACI) is attached; the 0x69 block is inline below in case
the attachment is stripped.

On the GUIDs: I take your point that they may be sample code rather
than Acer-assigned. Either way, the board exposes ABBC0F6B/6C/6D
(\_SB.WMI._WDG, dsdt.dsl:95736) and modinfo acer_wmi lists only
676AA15E/6AF4F258/67C3371D, so nothing in-tree binds here.

On SCMD 0x69, the dump supports the mechanism in my first mail, with
limits. Arg1 is a 4-bit mask and each set bit writes its value (1-4)
to EC register 0xFF (dsdt.dsl:97197; the same body is in
\_SB.DCHU.SCMD and inline in \_SB.WMI.CPKG and \_SB.DCHU.CPKG,
driven there by ARGP[0xBA]):

If ((ToInteger (Arg1) =3D=3D 0x69))
{
    If (^^PC00.LPCB.EC.ECOK)
    {
        Local4 =3D ARGS
        If ((Local4 & One))
        {
            ^^PC00.LPCB.EC.ECMD (Buffer (0x08)
                {
                     0x03, 0x00, 0xC1, 0xFF, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00
                })
        }
        ... same for (Local4 & 0x02/0x04/0x08)
        ... writing 0x02/0x03/0x04 to register 0xFF
    }
    Local0 =3D 0x69
    Return (Local0)
}

The selector mechanism looks real to me, but: nothing in the
firmware names the modes, so quiet/balanced/performance/gaming are
my labels, unconfirmed; and nothing reads 0xFF back (the DSDT's
ECMD reads cover registers 0x00-0x05 only), so Linux can only track
its last write. Measured effect is also thinner than I claimed:
across the tested settings and 2/4/8-core loads to 30 s, fan 1 sat
at ~2700 RPM at 94-96 C with no separation between settings, and
the only ramp ever seen was ~4900 RPM in one uncontrolled run.

So I am asking only for the narrow change: the three GUIDs in
acer_wmi_id_table[] so the driver binds. The 0xFF selector is
reported above for your judgment on whether any platform_profile
wiring is warranted.

On the chatbot question: yes. AI assisted the DSDT analysis, with
each register write verified live on the machine; the 0x69 reading
above matches the dump. Same on the backlight: with no driver
binding, I used TUXEDO clevo_wmi to confirm the protocol (bypassing
its DMI gate), then forked just the keyboard path into a self-owned
module.

Happy to re-spin as GUID-only, or drop it if sample-code GUIDs
aren't worth claiming. Your call either way.

Thanks,
Muhammed Anas=C2=A0


